# Scratch data stays out, but every cross-language parity fixture is checked in:
# each one is required by BOTH a Scala suite and a Rust test, and the whole point is
# that neither needs the other language installed.
#
# The negation is by *pattern*, not a per-directory list, and that is deliberate. The
# list version meant a new fixture needed a line here, and forgetting it fails in the
# worst available way: the file is present locally, so every test passes for whoever
# generated it and the suite is simply absent for everyone else. That went unnoticed
# once already. Naming a fixture directory `<name>-parity` now makes it tracked with
# no edit to this file.
#
# Residual: a fixture directory NOT matching `*-parity` is still silently ignored. If
# you add one, either follow the convention or verify with
#   git check-ignore -v test-data/<dir>/<file>
# which prints nothing when the file will be committed.
test-data/*
!test-data/*-parity/
# Anchored: an unanchored `bin/` also swallows rust/src/bin/, whose contents are
# real source.
